  [1mEvery access is wrapped.[0m Touching [38;5;153mwindow.localStorage[39m can throw outright — [38;5;153mSecurityError[39m when a browser or embedded
  webview blocks storage, quota errors on write — so [38;5;153msession.js[39m guards each call rather than feature-detecting once at
  load. Availability isn't a stable property; a write can fail on a browser where a read just succeeded.

  [1mFailure policy differs by direction, deliberately:[0m

  - [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m [1mnever throws[0m. Anything it can't produce a valid record from — storage blocked, key absent, parse
    failure, wrong shape — returns [38;5;153mnull[39m, i.e. "nobody is logged in." That's the safe reading of a failed identity read,
    and it means no caller ever needs a try/catch.
  - [38;5;153msetSession()[39m [1mreturns a boolean[0m for whether the write landed, and the submit handler warns to the console when it's
    [38;5;153mfalse[39m. This is the case I don't want swallowed: if the write fails, the user is logged in on this page and will be a
    stranger again after a reload. Silently ignoring that produces a bug report ("it keeps forgetting me") with nothing
    in the logs to explain it.

  [1mCorrupt data self-heals.[0m If the key holds unparseable JSON, or valid JSON of the wrong shape (a bare string, [38;5;153mnull[39m, an
  object missing [38;5;153muserId[39m), [38;5;153mgetSession()[39m clears the key and returns [38;5;153mnull[39m. The alternative — leave it and keep failing —
  means one bad write bricks the app for that user on every subsequent load, with no UI to recover. A record is valid
  only if [38;5;153muserId[39m and [38;5;153musername[39m are both non-empty strings.

  [1mNothing is cached in memory.[0m Every read goes to storage. This costs nothing at this scale and buys the cross-tab case
  for free: log out in one tab, and the next read in another tab already sees it gone. That's specifically why I'm
  comfortable with you having ruled out the subscription mechanism — without a cache, there's no stale copy for a
  subscription to correct. It only falls short for a page sitting idle that never reads again, which no current code
  does.

  [1mOne thing that isn't an edge case — it's a constraint the spec will state outright:[0m this stored userId is [1mnot [0m
  [1mauthentication[0m. [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is fully editable by the user; anyone can set [38;5;153muserId[39m to any value. It's fine as "who this
  browser thinks it is" for prefilling and display. The natural next step once a backend appears — send the stored
  userId up and trust it to identify the user — would be an authentication bypass. The real backend must derive identity
  from a credential it issued and verifies, never from this field. I'd rather have that written down now than
  discovered later, since the whole point of this design is that other code will start depending on the value.

  Does this section look right?

